<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="octanoyl-[acyl-carrier protein] biosynthesis (mitochondria, yeast)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7388" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Fatty acid biosynthesis in eukaryotic organisms is carried out in independent, dual cellular locations, the cytosol with fatty acid synthase (FAS) complexes (FAS I type) synthesizing fatty acids |CITS:[6137188] [16950653]| (for example |FRAME:PWY-5994|) and the mitochondria where monofunctional enzymes encoded in the yeast nucleus catalyze the various steps of fatty acid biosynthesis (FAS II type) |CITS:[19028688] [9187370]|. The latter resembles the bacterial FAS II system (see |FRAME:PWY0-501|, |FRAME:PWY0-1275|) but except for |FRAME:CPD-195|, the precursor for |FRAME:LIPOIC-ACID| |CITS:[9218413]|, the functions of other final products made by the pathway remain unclear and require further research |CITS:[19028688]|. It has been speculated that the main purpose of mitochondrial fatty acid biosynthesis lies in the maintenance of mitochondria |CITS:[11509667]| and respiration |CITS:[16246072]| which is closely connected to |FRAME:LIPOIC-ACID| biosynthesis and incorporation |CITS:[9037098] [9218413] [18779316]| but may also contribute fatty acids as constituent of mitochondrial phospholipid membranes |CITS:[Schneider95]|. In addition, novel links have been proposed to exist between mitochondrial fatty acid synthesis and RNA processing where products from the mitochondrial FAS II pathway are required for RNase P activity in yeast which in turn is controlled by positive feedback mechanisms |CITS:[18779316] [19686777]|. The mitochondrial fatty acid biosynthesis involves an initiation sequence (see |FRAME: PWY66-429|) and an elongation sequence that involves four enzymes and produces an acyl-[acyl carrier protein] (acyl-[acp]). In each cycle the acyl-[acp] product is condensed with |FRAME:MALONYL-ACP &quot;malonyl-[acp]&quot;| formed in the initiation pathway, resulting in its elongation by two carbons. The cycle is repeated three times until |FRAME: Octanoyl-ACPs &quot;octanoyl-[acp]&quot;| is formed |CITS:[18779316]|. The last enzyme of the initiation pathway (|FRAME: YER061C|), which produces |FRAME:Acetoacetyl-ACPs &quot;acetoacetyl-[acp]&quot;|, participates in both initiation and elongation, as the reaction it catalyzes is also the first of the 4 elongation steps - the condensation of a substrate with |FRAME:MALONYL-ACP &quot;malonyl-[acp]&quot;|. The subsequent reduction to |FRAME:Beta-3-hydroxybutyryl-ACPs| by the 3-oxoacyl-[acyl-carrier-protein] reductase (|FRAME: YKL055C|) |CITS:[9388293] [14562095]| is followed by the dehydration reaction that produces |FRAME:Crotonyl-ACPs| |CITS:[15387819]|. The enzyme catalyzing the dehydration (|FRAME: G3O-31118|) differs from the prior enzymes in the pathway by a lack of homology to the corresponding prokaryotic enzyme. The enoyl-[acyl-carrier-protein] reductase (|FRAME: G3O-29006|) catalyzes the formation of the end product of the first cycle, |FRAME:Butanoyl-ACPs butanoyl-[acp]|. This enzyme also shows no obvious homology to its bacterial counterpart |CITS:[11509667][12614607]|. The cycle repeats itself until |FRAME: Octanoyl-ACPs &quot;octanoyl-[acp]&quot;| is produced. This product serves as the precursor for |FRAME:LIPOIC-ACID| biosynthesis (see |FRAME:PWY-7382|.</Description>
